<<~ ahu #/what-travels>>

! Federating a Vessel

A Lararium runs as a ''swarm of one operator's vessels'' (a PersonaGroup) — each device an equal peer, each holding the operator's bags, syncing as a causal island. This carrier walks how a //second// vessel (say, a QA-lab box) joins the swarm.

''What github carries'' (public, shared by clone):
* the source, and
* the tracked genesis seed — `genesis/seed.json` and `genesis/cas/`, the baked bundle every vessel shares (engine + memes; no private identity).

''What NEVER enters github'' (identity-bearing — each lives in the vessel's own data home, outside the clone):
* `<lares>/identity/.vessel-key*.json` — this vessel's OWN private signing key. The device mints it and it never leaves the device.
* `<lares>/vessel/social-bootstrap.json` — the PersonaGroup pointers (admin/identities/circles/sessions doc-URLs + the sentinel PersonaGroup / MeshCabal IDs). Each vessel writes its own, at founding or at join.

''What crosses between two vessels'': the joinee's PUBLIC verifying key one way, and the signed admit payload the other (`device-admit/v1` — the device-delegation edge, the pinned persona-KEL chain, the PersonaGroup secret sealed to the joinee's key, and the doc pointers). The signed edge binds the joinee's key to the hearth's True Name and the sealed secret carries its own signature, so a hostile carrier may withhold either and forge neither; the remaining payload fields ride as untrusted carriage. The payload rides whatever channel the operator already trusts — a file, a paste, a QR — never the public remote. A clone alone cannot join a PersonaGroup; joining requires what the founding vessel hands over deliberately.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/two-models>>

!! The True Name Model ~ and the stopgap it retires

> ''THE TRUE NAME MODEL HOLDS; the copy-the-key stopgap is RETIRED'' (operator ruling). Copying one key across vessels names the ''antipattern'' the multi-device literature warns against — SSB-fusion and Veilid both copy-the-key and both lose per-device revocation; Keybase built its per-device model precisely to escape "one PGP key everywhere." The pono target rides the ''two-axis model'': each vessel mints its OWN distinct key — the ''user×vessel bond'' — delegated into the operator ''PersonaGroup'' by a signed edge. The signed-delegation edge composes up the ''SOCIAL axis'' `device-vessel → PersonaGroup → CabalGroup` (who reads); above it, ''NexusGroup'' and ''DreamNet'' federate across the ''INFRA axis'' by treaty / mutual carriage-contract (who relays), not by delegation-up — the two axes cross at the vessel (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/two-axis-topology">>). The key is the node; the ''delegation is the relationship''. The identity model — the two axes, the per-vessel key, the delegation edge — stands whole at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity">>; its mesh topology at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture">>.

''THE RETIRED STOPGAP — one operator identity copied across devices.'' Every vessel carries the //same// operator key, so every vessel presents the //same// DID — already a member of the PersonaGroup. The QA box receives the operator key and a device-admit payload out-of-band, loads them, and boots as the same operator on another device.

''THE TRUE NAME MODEL — a distinct key per vessel, delegated in at the hearth's True Name.'' Each vessel forges its //own// key, and ''the joinee mints first'': for the `device-admit` mint, only its PUBLIC verifying key crosses to the founder. The founder's PersonaGroup root signs a root→joinee ''device-delegation edge'' over that key and the hearth's True Name, and in the same enrolment seals the PersonaGroup secret to that device key (`lares device-admit --joinee-key <hex>` → `runDeviceAdmit` → `runDeviceAdmitEdge`, `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/ceremony-core.ts`). The joinee verifies the edge at its Binding Gate against the pinned persona-KEL signer. This path stands BUILT: it carries no Keyhive cap events, waits on no Beelay, and the verb refuses without the joinee's key. Boot holds no membership gate for a fresh key to fail — it proves identity alone (#/the-gate).

''The ground of the model'' (operator ruling 2026-06-23): ''roll our OWN per-vessel keying'' rather than wait on the pre-alpha Rust branch. As measured at that ruling, a joining device needs ''two'' things to read a Keyhive group, and Keyhive's JS surface delivers only the first:

# the ''key-chain + membership'' (CGKA + delegation ops) — JS-available via `eventsForAgent` / ingest; necessary but not sufficient; and
# the encrypted ''Document content'' itself — which Keyhive moves via ''sedimentree'' (content-addressed strata sync) under ''Beelay'' (the auth-sync RPC; zero-knowledge server). ''Both stood Rust-only at that measure — absent from the `keyhive_wasm` JS surface'' (the `automerge/beelay` repo lay dead; the live code sat in `beelay-core` inside the keyhive monorepo), and the keyhive repo carried the cross-peer admit + offline-device work as open PRs (#205 prekey-archives / offline-admit, #116 doc-sync listener, #110 reachable-docs).

So a join that waited on Keyhive's own document sync would never read the group. The house holds its own public-events-and-ciphertext crossing (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/persona-crossing.ts`), and the `device-admit` payload itself carries the delegation edge and the sealed PersonaGroup secret, never content. A distinct per-vessel key gains admission by a signed delegation edge; the design then has the joinee decrypt WITHOUT Beelay by ingesting only ''PUBLIC bytes'' — the CGKA / membership ops plus the ciphertext — because keyhive's add-member keys the content to the joinee's OWN leaf. A probe on `@keyhive/keyhive@0.1.0-alpha.6` proved that reading (the house pins `0.1.0-alpha.8`): a DISTINCT-identity device reads the group from public bytes + its own prekey secret, ''no sealed box, no secret in transit'' for that content crossing — it carries transport, not crypto (the admit payload's sealed PersonaGroup secret rides apart from it). keyhive read runs ''FORWARD-ONLY'' (a member reads content encrypted at or after its add), so the founder ADDs then ENCRYPTs, and grants a new member ''pre-existing'' content by RE-ENCRYPTing each chunk (route A — still public ops + ciphertext). Only the `*Keyed` shortcut (route B — hand the joinee a 32-byte application secret) ships a secret and demands a sealed channel; the default never does. The PROPOSED four-port swap surface names where Beelay slots in later with low churn (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity#/four-port-swap">>, #encrypt-from-start). Per-vessel keying stands built, so ''the True Name Model holds'' and the stopgap stands retired. Track under <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/mesh-governance">>.

The procedure below enacts ''the retired stopgap'' — kept as the witnessed record of how a second vessel joined before per-vessel keying, never as the path to walk.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/three-axes>>

!! Three Axes ~ one inversion of control

The SOCIAL/INFRA cut names two axes; a third rides beside them, and the three answer three orthogonal
questions. ''INFRA'' asks //who RELAYS// (Nexus / DreamNet, by treaty / carriage-contract — route through,
never trust). ''SOCIAL'' asks //who READS// (`vessel → PersonaGroup → CabalGroup`, by signed Keyhive
delegation — boolean, present-tense, revocable). ''REPUTATION'' asks //how much you WEIGH// (the vouch-DAG,
by a seed-rooted ε-fold — scalar, accumulated, sybil-bounded; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity#/the-lineage-fold">>).

The three never collapse into one because no axis derives another: a member READS a cabal (holds the cap)
yet WEIGHS little (a shallow lineage), or weighs heavily yet sits revoked. Authorization runs
forgery-resistant (a delegation never forges); reputation runs sybil-resistant (standing never forges,
because minting keys splits mass — the bottleneck property). Different threat models, different math,
different temporal grain.

''SOCIAL splits again — self ⊥ org — and each admission runs its own flow.'' The who-reads chain
`vessel → PersonaGroup → CabalGroup` folds TWO concerns the gate keeps apart. ''SOCIAL/self'' — a vessel
joining its own human's PersonaGroup — runs SINGLE-principal: you admit your own device, so no vouch, no
price, only a delegation and the sealed document handoff (the crossing). ''ORG'' — a member joining a
shared CabalGroup — runs MULTI-principal and carries MULTIPLICITY: one member joins many cabals, each its
own Circle with its own founders, its own invite policy, its own vouch-lineage, so your weight in cabal A
never transfers to cabal B. Three admission flows fall out, and they stay distinct: ''(1) vessel →''
''PersonaGroup'' (self, no vouch — the envelope handoff); ''(2) user → DreamNet'' (the outer gate,
invite-only-at-first, vouch-seeded at the mesh root); ''(3) member → cabal(s)'' (per-cabal, multiplicity).
Flows 2 and 3 ride the SAME instruments (`cabal-invite` + `admission-price` + `vouch-dag`) and differ ONLY
by SCOPE — the `placeDocId` and the lineage `seed` — so the machinery composes across scales without the
flows collapsing into one. Flow 1 stands apart: a distinct mechanism (delegation + envelope), never the
vouch machinery.

''The through-line — one move made three times: control inverts to the edge, never a root.'' A Circle "holds
what mutual bindings declare" (the Kowloon / group-as-closure model, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle#/composition">>):
no root grants membership (the SOCIAL graph stands up bottom-up from the vessels' own signed edges), no
authority assigns standing (the REPUTATION graph folds from the vouches the peers themselves signed), no
relay owns you (the INFRA graph rides mutual carriage-treaty). ''The key is the node; the delegation is the
relationship.'' The ''Vault'' seals the inversion at the top — the collector that knows "these handles are
one human" stays the user's private secret, never a published graph, riding no wire
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle#/the-vault">>).

''Two acts, not one — the vouch stays separable.'' A ''CabalGroup'' reads as a multi-principal Circle
(several humans' PersonaGroups mutually bound). Joining it fires an AUTHORIZATION act — a Keyhive
delegation, //"this vessel may now read the cabal."// A VOUCH — //"I stake my standing that this peer//
//belongs"// — rides as a SEPARATE act, and the two never fuse: a member you let read may carry no stake, a
peer you stake on may hold no cap. PGP keeps exactly this split — key-validity (public, exported) in one
store, owner-trust (private, never exported) in another, and one signs many keys at owner-trust zero.
Folding "I delegated to you" into "I vouch for you" names the confused-deputy bug (Miller, ocap), never a
feature. So the REPUTATION fold (`lineage-rank`) never derives its edges from the delegation log — it folds
the EXPLICIT vouches (the `CabalInvite` stake) alone. Storage MAY still share: a vouch MAY ride a delegation
record as an optional stake weight, but only as a separable dimension defaulting OFF, so access stands at
vouch=0 and the reputation read never shadows every grant. Both still invert to the user — the peers sign
their own edges, and no registry holds them.

The inversion runs pure but for ONE shore: the multi-principal
key-handover inside a CabalGroup. A single-principal me-Circle (your own devices) needs no tie-break — you
hold the one ordering authority. A CabalGroup of several humans constitutes the smallest ordering seat for
itself (an elected kahu, or accept-and-heal) — the one place a Circle stands up a thin constitutional core,
and it stays the operator's seat (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle#/composition">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/procedure>>

!! The Procedure (the retired stopgap, witnessed 2026-06-21)

''On the founding vessel'' — mint a clean admit payload (sentinel IDs + the group's signed cap-events):

```
lares device-admit --out admit.json
```

(The verb as built refuses this line: it demands `--joinee-key <hex>`, the joining vessel's PUBLIC verifying key, and it signs a delegation edge over that key where the stopgap payload carried cap-events for a shared one.)

Transmit ''out-of-band'' to the new vessel: `admit.json`, and the operator key (`.lararium/.vessel-key-*.json`). Never the public remote.

''On the new vessel'' — bootstrap the CLI once (it cannot build itself into existence), place the operator key under `<LAR_ROOT>/.lararium/`, then stand up and join:

```
pnpm install && pnpm -r build      # bootstrap the lares CLI + node
lares vessel stand --admit admit.json      # idempotent join-standup
```

`lares vessel stand --admit` runs the same found-if-absent standup as `--install` — build, mempalace, genesis (skipped; `island.bin` rides the clone) — but the init step ''joins'' the PersonaGroup from the payload instead of founding a new one: it loads the operator key (same DID = a member), applies the cap-events, writes the local `social-bootstrap.json` into the vessel's own store, and stands the node up. The keyhive boot gates then verify its identity and the vessel goes live. Re-running skips the init step (the bootstrap already lives).

This procedure records the retired copied-key flow and cannot serve as instructions for the built admit path. The flag itself stands built and keeps the same found-if-absent shape (`packages/lares-cli/src/standup.ts`): given an admit payload, the init step joins under the vessel's OWN keypair into the same group; a present bootstrap skips the init ceremony while `stand` still performs its remaining steps. The clone carries `genesis/seed.json` and `genesis/cas/` where this record names `island.bin`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-gate>>

!! The Gate, Honestly

A joining vessel proves its IDENTITY on every boot, and only its identity: its DID matches its key (Gate A), and a device delegation signed by its persona KEL's head key names this vessel's key (the Binding Gate). Cabal affiliation never gates boot. A PersonaGroup belongs to many Nexuses or none, so Gate C — the PersonaGroup's membership in the Nexus MeshCabal — belongs at ACCESS to the cabal's shared canon (`boot-daemon-keyhive.ts`; ''owed in code'': no access path reads it yet), and it checks cabal MEMBERSHIP, never the kahu quorum's seats. Under the retired stopgap every gate passed trivially because the vessel presented the same operator's own key — exactly the merge the True Name Model refuses. Under the True Name Model, the Binding Gate fails until a delegation brings the vessel's OWN key in, so the contact-card exchange counts not as a delay but as ''the gate doing its work'': the vessel must gain admission as itself.

A vessel never joins by pulling a repo; it joins by receiving — deliberately, out-of-band — what the founder hands it. The public clone carries the house; the operator carries the key. The founder hands the delegation, and the grant hop stands as authenticated as the ceremony it rides: whatever travels beside the grant binds by a digest into the persona-signed transcript. In the ruled model a member reads a sealed body through its bag's content key, which a lockbox carries to the bag's key group, and no admission delivers a read key; the lockbox store stands owed, and the code's keyring delivery at admission leaves (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/key-delivery">>).

A ''browser'' vessel meets one more gate before any of this — the ''secure-context wall'': `crypto.subtle` + OPFS demand `https`/`localhost`, so a phone on `http://192.168.x.x` cannot mint its key. A DNS-01 wildcard on the kahu-owned domain clears it with zero per-device install (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/windows>>

!! The Windows Surface

A QA vessel on native Windows 11 (PowerShell, not WSL) stands up through the same `lares vessel stand --install` / `--admit`, with these surface notes:

* ''`lares` global'' — `--install`/`--admit` writes a `lares.cmd` shim beside the bin (on Unix it symlinks into `~/.local/bin`). Add that directory to PATH so `lares` runs bare. The link step stays self-contained — it does not depend on `pnpm setup`/`PNPM_HOME`.
* ''Python'' — the integration check resolves `python3` → `python` → `py`, so the check finds Windows' `python`/`py`.
* ''The wake hook stays portable'' — it runs node exec-form (`node lares-wake-hook.mjs`), no shell, identical on Windows and Unix.
* ''mempalace's keep-hooks run in bash'' (`Stop` / `PreCompact`) — on native Windows they need ''Git for Windows'' (which Claude Code already requires for its bash tool). With Git Bash present, `lares vessel stand --claude` wires them as `bash "<path>"`; without it, the verbatim keep-leg stays dark until someone installs Git for Windows.
* ''`lares vessel stand --restart`'' (the dev restart) checks POSIX ports with `lsof` then `ss`, and Windows ports with `netstat`; the restart path signals the discovered process (`packages/lares-cli/src/port-control.ts`).

